{
  "dom_id": "step-q48338",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "The area of a square increases at a rate of $25{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the lengths of the sides of the square increasing when the sides are $2\\text{ cm}$ long?",
  "worked_solution": "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14187261/assets/a2bd105cbf7aa6fc2af8b2189e63122acfbb0bcf70b007cd35efd8492d273dca.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =25\\text{ cm}{}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{25}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when the sides are $2\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $x=2.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{25}{2\\cdot (2)}=\\frac{25}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the lengths of the sides are increasing at a rate of $\\frac{25}{4}\\,\\text{cm/s}.$",
